1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:05,612
Before the algorithm chooses anything, the city has to know where a tree can actually go.

2
00:00:05,562 --> 00:00:09,175
CoolSpend never optimizes over open space.

3
00:00:09,175 --> 00:00:16,737
It first builds a finite set of candidate slots — each one a single, pre-validated spot a tree could physically occupy.

4
00:00:16,737 --> 00:00:29,150
It starts on a four-metre lattice, then tests every point against the plantability constraints: inside the site, off the buildings, off the roadway, clear of every façade, and at least eight metres from any other tree.

5
00:00:29,150 --> 00:00:39,587
That eight-metre spacing isn't a guess — it's where three standards converge: NACTO's six-to-nine, the climate guideline's eight-to-ten, de-paving practice's six-to-ten.

6
00:00:39,587 --> 00:00:42,012
Then each slot picks a mode.

7
00:00:42,012 --> 00:00:48,325
A planter sits on pavement — contained roots, just point-six metres off a wall, no digging.

8
00:00:48,325 --> 00:00:59,987
In-ground means a de-paved pit, roots in real soil — a larger mature crown, so more cooling, but a six-metre foundation setback and a buried-utility risk the system won't pretend it can see.

9
00:00:59,987 --> 00:01:05,412
So every in-ground slot ships with one honest flag: requires utility survey.

